#|
Kanata configuration for a Brazilian ABNT2 keyboard on Linux. Ctrl also lives
on Caps Lock and is remapped at the kernel level, so kanata sees it as a
regular left Ctrl and passes it through untouched. Esc, Alt (the i3 mod key)
and AltGr are not remapped at all.

The home row carries the modifiers: ctrl on a and on the c-cedilla key, alt on
s and l, and shift on f and j. The d and k keys are layer keys instead.
Tapping either one types the letter as usual, holding d switches to the num
layer (a numpad on the right hand and brackets on the left hand) and holding
k switches to the sym layer (programming symbols).

Kanata works with physical key codes, so every symbol below is defined by the
physical key that types it on an ABNT2 layout. The operating system layout
must therefore be br with the abnt2 variant, otherwise the symbols will come
out wrong. Key names in the comments use US names, which describe the physical
position, and a few of those keys print a different character on ABNT2.
|#

(defcfg
  #|
  Also process keys that are not listed in defsrc. Without this, the tap-hold
  keys below would ignore unmapped keys, such as Shift, Space and the digits,
  when deciding between tap and hold.
  |#
  process-unmapped-keys yes
)

#|
The ABNT2 layout has two keys that a US keyboard does not have: the backslash
and vertical bar key to the left of Z, and the slash and question mark key to
the left of the right Shift. They are given custom names here, built from
their Linux evdev key codes, which are 86 (KEY_102ND) and 89 (KEY_RO).
|#
(deflocalkeys-linux
  abntbs    86
  abntslash 89
)

#|
Only the three letter rows, plus the key right of P, are remapped. Every other
key passes through unchanged thanks to process-unmapped-keys.
|#
(defsrc
  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [
  a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    scln
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Timeouts in milliseconds. The first number of a tap-hold action is the
tap-repress timeout (how soon a repeated press right after a tap still counts
as a tap, which matters for double letters and for repeated keys such as j and
k in vim). The second one is the hold timeout. The values are on the long side
because of a heavy typing style: a key held longer than the hold timeout
turns into its modifier or layer. Weaker and slower fingers (pinky and ring)
get a longer hold timeout than the index fingers, and the layer keys have
their own value. Because the home row mods use tap-hold-release, the hold
also starts as soon as another key is pressed and released, so a longer
timeout barely slows down shortcuts. If a letter still gets lost or a
modifier fires by accident, adjust these in steps of 20.
|#
(defvar
  tap        200
  hold-pinky 350
  hold-ring  330
  hold-index 300
  hold-layer 300
)

(defalias
  ;; The home row mods use tap-hold-release, which resolves to the hold action
  ;; when another key is pressed and released while this key is still down,
  ;; or when the hold timeout expires. The scln key is the c-cedilla key on
  ;; ABNT2.
  a    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-pinky a lctl)
  s    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-ring  s lalt)
  f    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-index f lsft)
  j    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-index j rsft)
  l    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-ring  l lalt)
  scln (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-pinky scln rctl)

  ;; The d and k keys are layer keys. A tap types the letter and a hold
  ;; activates the layer for as long as the key stays down. They use
  ;; tap-hold-release so that fast rolls such as "do" or "key" still type
  ;; letters instead of switching layers.
  d    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-layer d (layer-while-held num))
  k    (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-layer k (layer-while-held sym))

  ;; From here on, every alias is named after the character it types on an
  ;; ABNT2 layout. The first ones are shifted characters from the digit row.
  ex   S-1       ;; !
  at   S-2       ;; @
  hsh  S-3       ;; hash sign
  dol  S-4       ;; $
  pct  S-5       ;; %
  amp  S-7       ;; &
  ast  S-8       ;; *
  lpar S-9       ;; (
  rpar S-0       ;; )
  und  S--       ;; _
  pls  S-=       ;; +
  col  S-/       ;; colon, since the physical slash key prints a semicolon

  ;; Brackets live on unusual keys on ABNT2: the physical ] key types [ and
  ;; the physical backslash key types ].
  lbkt ]         ;; [
  rbkt bksl      ;; ]
  lbrc S-]       ;; {
  rbrc S-bksl    ;; }

  ;; These four aliases use the custom key names defined in deflocalkeys-linux,
  ;; for the keys that only exist on ABNT2.
  bsl  abntbs            ;; backslash
  pipe S-abntbs          ;; vertical bar
  sl   abntslash         ;; slash
  qst  S-abntslash       ;; question mark

  ;; The key left of 1 types an apostrophe, and a double quote when shifted.
  sq   grv       ;; apostrophe
  dq   S-grv     ;; double quote

  ;; Tilde, circumflex and backtick are dead keys on ABNT2, so they only
  ;; appear after the next key press. Each macro types the dead key followed
  ;; by a space, which produces the plain character on its own.
  til  (macro apos 10 spc)      ;; tilde
  car  (macro S-apos 10 spc)    ;; circumflex
  bt   (macro S-[ 10 spc)       ;; backtick
)

(deflayer base
  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [
  @a   @s   @d   @f   g    h    @j   @k   @l   @scln
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Hold d to get this layer, which puts a numpad on the right hand and brackets
on the left hand. On the left hand the ring finger types the opening brackets
and the index finger types the closing ones: w, s and x give [, ( and {, while
r, f and v give ], ) and }. On the right hand, u, i and o give 7, 8 and 9, j, k
and l give 4, 5 and 6, and m, comma and period give 1, 2 and 3, with 0 on n and
the decimal point on h. The decimal comma, which is what Brazilian numbers use,
is on g, right next to the decimal point. The remaining right hand keys carry
the operators: y types =, p types *, the key right of p types /, the c-cedilla
key types + and the physical slash key types -. The d key does nothing inside
its own layer, which is why it is mapped to XX, a no-op.

The z key reloads this configuration file (live reload), so holding d and
pressing z applies any edit without restarting the service. If the new file
has an error, kanata keeps running with the previous configuration and
prints the error in its log.
|#
(deflayer num
  _    @lbkt _   @rbkt _    =    7    8    9    @ast @sl
  _    @lpar XX  @rpar ,    .    4    5    6    @pls
  lrld @lbrc _   @rbrc _    0    1    2    3    -
)

#|
Hold k to get this layer, which keeps programming symbols on the left hand and
on the outer keys of the right hand, because the right middle finger is busy
holding k and cannot reach i, k or comma. On the left hand, the top row types
the shifted digits ! @ # $ and %, the home row types { ( ) } followed by a
backtick on g, and the bottom row types a backslash, [, ], * and +. On the
right hand, y, u, o and p type ^, &, a vertical bar and ?, the home row keys h,
j and l and the c-cedilla key type -, =, _ and /, and n, m, period and the
physical slash key type ~, an apostrophe, a colon and a double quote.

The characters < > and ; are left out of this layer because they are already
easy to reach on the base layer, with Shift and comma, Shift and period, and
the semicolon key itself.
|#
(deflayer sym
  @ex  @at  @hsh @dol @pct @car @amp XX   @pipe @qst XX
  @lbrc @lpar @rpar @rbrc @bt -    =    XX   @und @sl
  @bsl @lbkt @rbkt @ast @pls @til @sq  XX   @col @dq
)
